{
  "slug": "dol-pbm-fee-disclosure-rule",
  "title": "DOL PBM Fee Disclosure Rule: Meaning for Workers",
  "metaDescription": "The DOL PBM fee disclosure rule takes effect Jan 4, 2027. Disclosures go to employer plan fiduciaries, not workers, and the rule sets no copay.",
  "h1": "What the DOL PBM Fee Disclosure Rule Means for Employer Drug Plans and Copays",
  "cardBlurb": "The Department of Labor's new rule requires pharmacy benefit managers to disclose compensation to large self-insured employer plan fiduciaries starting in 2027.",
  "introText": "The dol pbm fee disclosure rule does not change the prescription drug plans or copays that workers choose during 2026 open enrollment. Released by the [Employee Benefits Security Administration (EBSA)](https://www.dol.gov/agencies/ebsa) of the Department of Labor (DOL), the final regulation on [Improving Transparency Into Pharmacy Benefit Manager Fee Disclosure](https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/11/02/2026-22306/improving-transparency-into-pharmacy-benefit-manager-fee-disclosure) becomes effective January 4, 2027. It applies to plan years beginning on or after July 1, 2027.\n\nThe rule requires pharmacy benefit managers (PBMs) to disclose their direct and indirect compensation to the fiduciaries of large, self-insured group health plans. It does not mandate disclosures directly to workers, nor does it require employers to change their copays, deductibles, premiums, or drug formularies. The regulation governs fee transparency between commercial vendors and employer plan sponsors rather than establishing consumer price limits.\n\nWorkers comparing employer coverage for the upcoming calendar year will not see adjustments driven by this regulation. The initial disclosures will not reach plan fiduciaries until mid-2027 or 2028 depending on the plan year, and any future shift in copays depends on how employers use the fee data.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "What the Rule Changes for Workers During Open Enrollment",
      "body": "The final regulation does not alter the prescription drug plans, copayment tiers, or deductibles presented to workers during 2026 open enrollment. The rule takes effect on January 4, 2027, and its disclosure obligations apply only to plan years beginning on or after July 1, 2027. Furthermore, the regulation applies strictly to covered large self-insured plans, and its operative text creates no obligation for service providers or employers to send fee disclosures to plan participants. The rule sets no limits on copays, establishes no caps on coinsurance, and contains no requirements regarding plan premiums or covered drug lists.\n\nWhat the regulation changes is the level of compensation detail delivered to responsible plan fiduciaries who oversee covered employer health plans. Service providers of pharmacy benefit management services expecting $1,000 or more in direct or indirect compensation must provide written disclosures detailing their fees, manufacturer rebates, spread pricing, and pharmacy recoupment. Plan fiduciaries must receive this information in advance of signing, extending, or renewing a service contract, followed by semiannual or quarterly reporting.\n\nFor an employee selecting a workplace benefit package today, the terms displayed in open enrollment materials remain the operative terms. The regulation does not automatically lower out-of-pocket costs at the pharmacy counter, and it does not mandate refunds of pharmacy revenue to individual workers. Any eventual change in cost sharing would occur later and indirectly, if an employer uses the reported fee data to negotiate different contract terms with its PBM."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Which Employer Plans the Rule Covers",
      "body": "The regulation defines covered plans using specific employer size standards while explicitly excluding several categories of group health coverage. Under paragraph (b) of the rule, a covered plan is a group health plan offered by a specified large employer or that is a specified large plan, subject to two statutory exclusions. It excludes any group health plan in which all benefits are provided exclusively through an insurance policy issued by a health insurance issuer. It also excludes any plan established by a sponsor that is, or is affiliated with, a drug manufacturer, distributor, wholesaler, PBM, or rebate aggregator.\n\nFor plans maintained by a single employer, paragraph (m)(6) defines a specified large employer as an employer that employed an average of at least 100 employees on business days during the preceding calendar year and employs at least one worker on the first day of the calendar year. These counts follow the aggregation and employee-counting rules of Internal Revenue Code section 4980H. A specified large plan, defined in paragraph (m)(7), covers plans established or maintained by sponsors described in 29 U.S.C. 1002(16)(B)(ii) or (iii) that averaged at least 100 participants on business days during the preceding plan year.\n\nSmaller self-insured plans below these thresholds do not receive the detailed fee schedules mandated by this regulation. Under preamble section 1.2.1, smaller self-insured plans remain subject to the statutory exemption in the Employee Retirement Income Security Act of 1974 (ERISA) section 408(b)(2)(B), meaning their service providers must supply generalized statutory compensation disclosures. The Department stated that it is reserving action regarding fully insured health plans.\n\n| Plan type | Detailed disclosures under this rule | What applies instead |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Large self-insured employer plan | Yes, for plans meeting size tests under paragraph (b) | Detailed advance and semiannual written disclosures under 29 CFR 2550.408b-22 |\n| Smaller self-insured plan | No, exempt from this rule's detailed disclosures | Generalized statutory compensation disclosures under ERISA section 408(b)(2)(B) |\n| Fully insured plan | No, excluded when all benefits are provided through insurance | Department is reserving action; existing statutory rules continue to apply |\n| Plan sponsored by drug-supply-chain affiliate | No, excluded under paragraph (b)(2) | Excluded from this regulation's disclosure requirements |"
    },
    {
      "heading": "What PBMs Must Disclose to Plan Fiduciaries",
      "body": "The disclosure obligations apply to providers of pharmacy benefit management services and affiliated providers of brokerage and consulting services entering contracts where they reasonably expect $1,000 or more in direct or indirect compensation. The rule applies based on the functions performed, regardless of whether a company identifies itself as a pharmacy benefit manager. The disclosure must be delivered in writing to a responsible plan fiduciary with the authority to enter, extend, or renew the contract.\n\nUnder paragraphs (e) and (g) of the regulation, service providers must supply comprehensive data on services and compensation streams:\n\n- A detailed description of all pharmacy benefit management, consulting, and brokerage services provided to the plan.\n- Direct compensation received from the plan, reported both in the aggregate and broken down by individual service.\n- Indirect compensation, including manufacturer payments such as rebates, administration fees, alternative discounts, and other remuneration, reported in the aggregate and for each formulary drug, specifying amounts passed to the plan and amounts retained by the provider.\n- Spread compensation broken down by drug National Drug Code (NDC) and pharmacy channel (retail, mail order, and specialty), including arrangements steering patients toward affiliated pharmacies.\n- Pharmacy recoupment figures, including copay claw-back compensation, defined as the difference between a copayment or coinsurance paid by a participant to a pharmacy and the actual reimbursement paid to that pharmacy.\n- Price-protection agreements, formulary placement incentives, and explanations of why reasonably available therapeutic equivalent alternatives were omitted from the formulary.\n- Net price per fill by drug and pharmacy channel, or an objective methodology that allows the plan fiduciary to verify the calculation.\n- The fiduciary status of the service provider and a formal audit right permitting the plan to review provider records.\n\nAll dollar figures must be stated as actual dollar amounts, though good-faith estimates are permitted when exact figures cannot be determined. Covered service providers must provide disclosures in a machine-readable file upon request. Additionally, providers may not restrict the plan fiduciary's use of the disclosed information, except through reasonable confidentiality agreements for third parties, and cannot prevent sharing with legal counsel or regulatory agencies."
    },
    {
      "heading": "When the Disclosures Start",
      "body": "The Department published the final regulation in the Federal Register on November 2, 2026, following its signing on October 28, 2026, by Daniel Aronowitz, Assistant Secretary of EBSA. The rule establishes an effective date of January 4, 2027, but sets its applicability date to plan years beginning on or after July 1, 2027. The Department adopted this delayed applicability date to allow employers and service providers adequate time to renegotiate existing contracts and structure future arrangements.\n\nBy simple arithmetic on the Federal Register dates, the regulation does not apply to a standard calendar-year employer plan for its 2027 plan year. A calendar-year plan's first plan year beginning on or after July 1, 2027, starts on January 1, 2028. The earliest plans subject to the regulation will be non-calendar-year plans whose coverage years begin on or shortly after July 1, 2027.\n\nWhen the rule applies, initial disclosures must be provided reasonably in advance of entering, extending, or renewing a service contract. For contract extensions and renewals, the rule deems 90 calendar days in advance to be a reasonable timeframe. Ongoing disclosures must follow on a semiannual basis, or quarterly upon the covered plan's request, delivered no later than 30 calendar days following the end of each reporting period. If a provider discovers an error, it must correct the disclosure within 30 calendar days of learning of the inaccuracy. These requirements operate separately from ERISA section 726, an amendment added by the Consolidated Appropriations Act, 2026, which mandates separate semiannual reporting on drug spending for plan years beginning on or after August 3, 2028."
    },
    {
      "heading": "How Disclosure Could Reach Copays Indirectly",
      "body": "Because the regulation imposes disclosure duties rather than drug pricing mandates, any effect on worker out-of-pocket spending depends entirely on employer action. In the preamble to the rule, the Department notes that in many self-insured plans, coinsurance charges are calculated based on a drug's list price rather than its net cost after manufacturer rebates. When a manufacturer increases a list price while offering larger rebates to the PBM, an employee paying 20 percent coinsurance pays a share of that elevated list price, even when the net cost to the plan is much lower.\n\nThe Department also observed that participants frequently pay the full cost of prescription drugs until satisfying their annual deductibles. PBMs market cost-control mechanisms to employers that can result in less favorable copayments or higher coinsurance for employees. Under the new rule, plan fiduciaries will see itemized copay claw-back figures, identifying instances where a worker's copay at the pharmacy counter exceeded the reimbursement the PBM actually remitted to the pharmacy.\n\nSeeing these figures does not trigger a direct refund to the worker who paid the copayment. The rule requires that claw-backs and retained manufacturer fees be disclosed to the employer's plan fiduciary, not returned to participants. An employer receiving this information could decide to renegotiate its contract, demand that rebates pass through to reduce cost sharing, or shift coinsurance calculations from list price to net price. For employees managing out-of-pocket medical costs through tax-advantaged accounts, reviewing [HSA tax advantages](/guides/hsa-triple-tax-advantage/) helps contextualize how personal savings interact with plan deductible structures."
    },
    {
      "heading": "What the Research Says About Costs",
      "body": "The Department of Labor and independent economic researchers present differing perspectives on whether transparency mandates reduce prescription spending, showing that marketwide outcomes remain uncertain. The Department expects that disclosing spread pricing, rebate retention, and formulary placement incentives will enable fiduciaries to attain more equitable patient cost sharing and broader medication access. In the regulatory impact analysis, the Department states that disclosures may help self-insured plans retain higher rebate shares, expand generic adoption, and lower out-of-pocket costs for participants filling prescriptions.\n\nIn section 8.1 of the regulatory text, the Department references an October 2024 report from the [Congressional Budget Office (CBO)](https://www.cbo.gov/system/files/2024-10/58793-rx-drug-prices.pdf). The CBO estimated that requiring PBMs to share drug price information with health issuers would reduce drug prices by 0.1 percent to 1.0 percent, noting that transparency helps smaller plans negotiate but produces limited overall savings because many contracts already include data sharing. Directly addressing this finding, the Department stated: \"the self-insured plans covered in this final rule is not subject to state disclosure laws, thus the final rule could have a bigger impact than CBO's estimates.\"\n\nA separate [CBO analysis of S. 1339](https://www.cbo.gov/system/files/2024-12/s1339.pdf), the Pharmacy Benefit Manager Reform Act, examined a different legislative proposal. CBO estimated that measure could reduce net retail drug spending by more than 0.5 percent in year one, lowering commercial premiums by less than 0.1 percent initially, with premium effects falling below 0.01 percent by 2034. CBO emphasized this slowed the rate of premium growth rather than causing premiums to decline.\n\nEmpirical research on state-level transparency mandates reveals mixed results. A study by Ginger Scanlon titled [Prescription for Savings? Disclosure in the Drug Market](https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=5021179) examined employer-sponsored claims from 2014 to 2022. Scanlon found that inter-firm disclosures most comparable to this rule increased prescription drug costs for plans by 3.5 percent while reducing participant out-of-pocket costs by 1 percent. In monopoly drug markets, patient costs declined by 9.4 percent with no significant impact on plan costs, whereas in competitive markets, plan costs rose without significant gains for workers. Conversely, a study of Medicare Part D data by Nicola Maria Fiore titled [Can Transparency Reduce Drug Costs](https://ssrn.com/abstract=5710162) found state PBM rebate disclosure laws did not reduce drug spending, premiums, deductibles, or out-of-pocket costs. The Department concluded that while disclosure mandates improve transparency, their direct effects on consumer drug spending remain uncertain."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Who This Is Not For",
      "body": "This regulation does not apply to all health plans, and workers in several categories of coverage will see no change in how their PBM operates. Employees covered by fully insured group health plans, where an employer purchases pre-packaged coverage from an insurance carrier, are excluded from the rule's detailed disclosures. Similarly, workers in smaller self-insured plans sponsored by businesses with fewer than 100 employees do not receive the detailed fee schedules established under paragraph (b).\n\nMedicare beneficiaries are also outside the scope of this rulemaking. Coverage for Medicare enrollees is governed by distinct federal statutes, and prescription drug costs for those plans are reviewed under separate standards. Individuals seeking information on Medicare drug benefit trends can consult the guide on [Medicare Part D 2027 premiums](/guides/medicare-part-d-2027-premiums/).\n\nFinally, this overview is an informational explainer for workers and plan participants, not legal, financial, or compliance advice for employers. Plan sponsors, fiduciaries, and benefits consultants seeking specific guidance on contract compliance, reporting standards, and fiduciary liability under ERISA should consult the full text of the rule in the Federal Register."
    },
    {
      "heading": "What Would Change This Answer",
      "body": "The conclusion that the rule does not alter current worker copays could change under specific circumstances in future plan years. The primary trigger for change is the calendar: once an employer's plan year begins on or after July 1, 2027, the disclosure mandates become legally enforceable against covered PBM service providers. For employers operating on non-calendar fiscal years starting in late 2027, disclosures will arrive months earlier than for calendar-year plans starting January 1, 2028.\n\nSecond, the outcome could change if an employer actively redesigns its prescription drug benefit after reviewing the newly disclosed data. If a plan fiduciary discovers substantial spread pricing or retained rebates and renegotiates the PBM contract to pass those savings through at the pharmacy counter, employee copays or coinsurance structures could shift.\n\nThird, administrative or judicial actions could alter the regulation's implementation. If federal agencies modify the regulatory text, publish supplementary guidance expanding coverage to fully insured plans, or alter implementation dates, the legal obligations described here would adjust accordingly."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Takeaway",
      "body": "For 2026 open enrollment, workers should treat the dol pbm fee disclosure rule as regulatory background rather than a factor affecting their immediate out-of-pocket health costs. When choosing coverage for 2027, compare each employer plan option based on its published copayment schedule, annual deductible, coinsurance rates, and formulary coverage. The regulation sets no copays, applies only to large self-insured plans, and delivers its fee disclosures to plan fiduciaries rather than individual workers starting in mid-2027 or 2028.\n\nThe economic evidence cited by the Department shows that long-term cost impacts remain uncertain. While the Department expects lower plan spending and potential out-of-pocket savings, and noted the rule could have a bigger impact than CBO's 0.1 to 1.0 percent price reduction estimate, independent researchers found conflicting results, including one study showing plan costs rose 3.5 percent while worker costs fell 1 percent. Workers weighing plan structures alongside flexible spending options can review the comparison between [HSA vs FSA](/compare/hsa-vs-fsa/) accounts. To verify how this regulatory timeline aligns with workplace benefits, ask your employer's human resources or benefits department whether your health plan is self-insured and what date its plan year begins."
    }
  ],
  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "What is a PBM?",
      "answer": "A pharmacy benefit manager (PBM) is a third-party intermediary hired by a health plan to manage its prescription drug benefit. The Department of Labor's rule defines PBM services functionally, covering entities that handle pharmacy networks, drug claims processing, rebate negotiation, and formulary administration, regardless of whether the company formally calls itself a PBM."
    },
    {
      "question": "Does the DOL PBM fee disclosure rule lower my copays?",
      "answer": "The rule does not directly lower worker copays. It imposes disclosure requirements on PBMs to report compensation to employer plan fiduciaries, but it contains no legal mandate setting or reducing copayments, coinsurance, or deductibles. Any change in worker costs depends on whether an employer renegotiates its contract after reviewing the fee disclosures."
    },
    {
      "question": "When does the DOL PBM fee disclosure rule take effect?",
      "answer": "The regulation takes effect on January 4, 2027, and applies to plan years beginning on or after July 1, 2027. For standard calendar-year employer plans, the first covered plan year begins on January 1, 2028, by simple arithmetic on the Federal Register dates."
    },
    {
      "question": "Does the rule apply to my employer's drug plan?",
      "answer": "The rule applies only to large self-insured group health plans. For a single-employer plan, the employer must have employed an average of at least 100 employees during the preceding calendar year. It does not apply to fully insured group health plans, smaller self-insured plans, or plans sponsored by entities affiliated with drug manufacturers, distributors, or PBMs."
    },
    {
      "question": "Will workers see the PBM disclosures?",
      "answer": "No. The regulation requires PBMs to provide written disclosures to responsible employer plan fiduciaries who have the authority to contract for plan services. The operative text contains no requirement to distribute fee schedules, rebate amounts, or spread pricing data directly to plan participants."
    },
    {
      "question": "What is spread pricing?",
      "answer": "Spread pricing occurs when a PBM charges an employer health plan a higher amount for a prescription drug than the reimbursement amount it pays to the pharmacy dispensing the medication, retaining the difference as compensation. The new rule requires PBMs to disclose this spread compensation broken down by drug National Drug Code and pharmacy channel."
    },
    {
      "question": "What is a copay claw-back?",
      "answer": "The regulation defines copay claw-back compensation as the difference between the copayment or coinsurance paid by a plan participant to a pharmacy and the lower reimbursement the PBM actually pays to that pharmacy. Under the rule, PBMs must disclose these recoupment amounts to plan fiduciaries."
    },
    {
      "question": "What is ERISA section 726?",
      "answer": "ERISA section 726 is a separate statutory provision added by the Consolidated Appropriations Act, 2026. It requires PBMs to provide semiannual drug spending reports to large self-insured plans starting with plan years on or after August 3, 2028, and mandates a summary document that plans can provide to participants upon request."
    }
